## Introduction

A mirror-smooth wall is not bought; it is built — through a sequence of preparation steps that each depend on the last. Skipping any one of them shows up later as ripple, pinholes, or paint that peels in sheets after the first rainy season.

This guide walks the full sequence for achieving a genuinely smooth wall finish on Nigerian blockwork: surface assessment, base coat application, progressive sanding, dust removal, priming, and the final coat technique — including the raking-light check professionals use to catch flaws before the paint does.

## Why Walls Go Wrong in the First Place

Understanding the four failure sources tells you exactly what the sequence is protecting against:

- **Suction.** Dry blockwork and plaster pull water out of coats applied over them too quickly. The binder never gets to cure properly, leaving a weak, chalky layer that looks fine until it fails.
- **Shrinkage.** Any coat applied thick — skim, filler, putty — shrinks as it dries. Beyond about 3 mm in one pass, the shrinkage shows up as hairline map-cracks and ridges at the edges.
- **Dust.** Sanding residue between coats blocks adhesion. A coat laid over dust is a coat laid over nothing.
- **Uneven coats.** Hand application naturally leaves ridges at stroke edges. Unmanaged, each new coat magnifies the last coat's texture instead of hiding it.

Every step below exists to neutralise one of these four. If you understand which step answers which threat, you will never be tempted to skip one, because each skip has a specific, predictable bill attached — payable months later.

## The Sequence, in Order

### 1. Assess and map the wall

Run a 1.5 to 2 m straightedge over the wall in vertical, horizontal, and diagonal passes. Mark every hollow and ridge with chalk, and tap-test for hollow-sounding plaster — those areas need cutting back and re-plastering, not smoothing over. The map you make now is the difference between fixing the wall and disguising it.

Record the findings per wall, not per room: one wall may need spot re-plastering while its neighbour needs nothing but putty. A typical hand-plastered room breaks down as: two walls with 2 to 4 mm ripple, one wall with a hollow patch near the window, and one wall that is genuinely fine. If your plan reads "skim everything the same way", re-plan.

### 2. Damp-cure the background

On absorbent blockwork and plaster, dampen the surface with clean water before applying cement-based coats. The wall should be dark with moisture but hold no free water. In hot weather, dampen again an hour before working. This single habit prevents more failures than any product you can buy: it slows suction so the coat cures instead of merely drying.

For gypsum work, the equivalent discipline is different: no dampening, but the wall must be genuinely dry, and the mix must be fresh. Gypsum sets chemically, and a batch that has started to stiffen in the bucket has already begun to lose strength that no amount of trowel skill will restore.

### 3. Apply the base coat thin — twice, not once

Whether the base is a cement-based level coat or gypsum skim, the rule is identical: two passes beat one thick pass.

- First pass: fill the hollows and bring the wall toward flat, roughly 2 to 3 mm maximum thickness. Leave it to firm.
- Second pass: flatten the whole surface, shaving ridges and filling what remains, with the trowel angle progressively reduced so the blade polishes rather than deposits.
- Feather every section boundary. Ridges you can feel with a fingernail will be visible under paint — and permanent.

Let the base coat dry and cure fully. Gypsum skims need roughly 24 to 48 hours of thorough drying; cement-based coats should be damp-cured for 2 to 3 days in hot weather, then dried.

**The trowel angle, explained.** A trowel held steep (about 60 degrees to the wall) deposits material — it pushes a bead of paste ahead of the blade. Held shallow (near flat, 10 to 20 degrees), the same blade shaves material off. Beginners apply and flatten with one angle and wonder why ridges remain. The discipline is: steep to load, progressively shallower to true, near-flat to polish. When the wall is right, the final passes move almost no material at all — they just wipe, and the sound of the blade changes to a hiss. That sound is the wall telling you it is flat.

### 4. Progressive sanding — grit order matters

Sand in stages, not in one assault:

1. **Medium grit (100–120)** first, to knock down ridges, trowel marks, and high spots. Use a flat sanding block or pole sander — loose paper in the hand digs grooves.
2. **Fine grit (180–220)** second, over the entire surface, to unify the texture.
3. **Very fine (240+)** only where a premium paint (satin, sheen) will read the texture; matte emulsion is more forgiving.
4. **Spot-check with the raking light between grits.** Angle a bright torch flat against the wall in dim light; shadows reveal every remaining ridge. Sand until the light reports silence.

Do not polish one patch and leave its neighbour rough — the paint will display the difference in sheen even where the touch cannot feel it. Sanding is a whole-wall, whole-room process, done in passes, not a spot treatment where your hand noticed something.

### 5. Dust removal — the step everyone skips

Vacuum the wall, or wipe it down with a sponge wrung nearly dry, and let it dry fully. Then putty the surface: one thin, scraped pass with a wide flex knife to fill pinholes and hairline pores, allow to dry, sand flush with fine grit, and dust again. The back-of-hand test is the standard: run a bare palm over the whole wall; any snag, grit, or ripple means repeat the pass. Your hand is more honest than your eye at this stage.

The economics of dust removal are absurdly one-sided. Wiping a room's walls costs twenty minutes. Skipping it means the primer and first coat bond to a film of sanded gypsum instead of the wall — and intercoat adhesion becomes the weak link for the entire life of the finish. The first humid season finds it; the peeling starts at exactly the places that were dustiest.

### 6. Prime

Primer equalises suction and bonds the layers above. On fresh skim and putty, use a sealer primer — or the paint manufacturer's recommended first-coat dilution — and let it dry fully. On previously painted walls, prime any sanded-through or repaired areas at minimum. Where primer is skipped, the first topcoat absorbs unevenly and the final sheen is patchy forever after.

Primer also acts as the lie detector for the stages before it: on a primed wall, the uniform matte film makes every remaining ripple and pinhole visible without the raking light. Look at the primed wall carefully before the first topcoat goes on — it is the last cheap moment.

### 7. Final coats with wet-edge discipline

- Cut in edges and corners by brush first.
- Roll in a "W" pattern per square metre, then fill without reloading, keeping a **wet edge** — always roll into paint that is still wet, never onto a drying boundary.
- Two thin coats, fully dried between, always beat one thick coat. Follow the manufacturer's recoat time; in Nigerian humidity, lean toward the longer end.
- Do the final wall in consistent lighting conditions. Raking daylight is the honest judge — walls finished under a single bulb always surprise their owners at noon.

## The Raking-Light Check — Inspect Like a Professional

The single habit that separates professional results is inspection with light held flat against the surface, in three passes:

1. **After the base coat sands** — catch ridges while they are still cheap.
2. **After putty** — confirm pinholes are gone before primer locks them in.
3. **After the first topcoat, before the last** — the last chance to fix without repainting everything.

A torch or work lamp held nearly parallel to the wall turns every defect into a shadow. Ten seconds per wall at each stage eliminates the six-month-later discovery. The technique works because the human eye is far better at judging edges and shadows than surfaces head-on: a ripple you cannot see from two metres away becomes a range of hills when the light grazes it.

## The Toolkit That Does the Job

The tools for a genuinely smooth wall are few and affordable (the [finishing tools guide](/learn/finishing-tools-every-contractor-should-own) covers buying them in detail):

- **Steel trowel (stainless, 250–300 mm)** — the applicator for skim; its edge condition prints on every wall.
- **Wide flex knife (300–400 mm)** — the putty tool; flexibility is the function.
- **Sanding block + pole sander with flat pads** — consistent backing prevents the hand's natural gullies.
- **Grit ladder: 100/120 → 180/220 → 240+** — never skip rungs.
- **Straightedge 1.5–2 m** — assessment and verification.
- **Bright torch** — the inspection instrument.
- **Bare hands** — the final gauge; wash them first.

A note on sandpaper economics: good paper cuts faster, clogs less, and leaves a cleaner scratch pattern than cheap paper. On a skim job the paper cost is trivial compared with the labour it protects — buy the branded sheets and change them when they glaze, not when they tear.

## A Worked Example — Bedroom Wall, Numbers Through the Sequence

Take a bedroom wall 4.2 m long × 2.7 m high: about 11.3 m², minus a 1.2 × 1.2 m window — roughly 10 m² net, plus reveal edges.

**Assessment:** straightedge shows 3 mm ripple in the upper half and a hollow 300 mm patch near the window corner that drums when tapped. Verdict: cut out and re-plaster the hollow patch first, then skim the full wall.

**Sequence with time budget:** Day 1 — cut back hollow, key edges, dampen, re-plaster patch; leave to cure. Days 2–3 — damp-cure the patch. Day 4 — dampen whole wall, skim pass one; firm up; skim pass two; leave overnight. Day 5 — sand 120 then 180, torch check, dust wipe, putty pass; leave overnight. Day 6 — fine sand putty, dust again, prime by midday. Day 7 — first topcoat. Day 8 — second topcoat. Torch check between every stage; the ridge the torch found on day 5 behind the door takes four minutes to fix, against a localized re-skim and blended repaint if it had survived to day 8.

**Materials (planning figures):** about 30 kg of gypsum skim (one 40 kg bag with repair margin), a 20 kg putty tub shared across several rooms, roughly 1 L of primer for this wall, and about 2 L of topcoat per two coats. Multiply across a whole flat with the [finishing estimator](/finish-estimator) instead of scaling by hand — ceilings, reveals, and different wall conditions make real totals diverge from simple multiplication quickly.

## Common Smooth-Finish Killers

| Symptom | Real cause | The fix that works |
|---|---|---|
| Ripple visible in evening light | Base coat not flattened; strokes not feathered | Re-skim thin, sand progressively, raking-light check |
| Pinholes everywhere after paint | Putty pass skipped or too thin | Putty + fine sand + reprime before recoating |
| Peeling sheets after one rainy season | Skim/paint over damp wall or no primer | Dry the wall properly; strip, prime, redo |
| Hairline map cracks | One thick skim pass; fast drying in sun | Thin passes; damp-cure cement coats |
| Patchy sheen | Suction unsealed (no primer) | Seal, then full recoat |
| Rough patches like fine sandpaper | Sanding dust painted over | Wash/dust, reprime, recoat |

## Working With the Season, Not Against It

The sequence assumes sane coating weather, and in Nigeria that is mostly a dry-season activity with harmattan caveats: fine dust is a coating contaminant, so close the room, cover buckets, and wipe surfaces immediately before each coat. In the rainy season, every drying interval stretches — double the patience on recoat times and keep windows open for ventilation between showers. Never coat a wall the morning after overnight rain soaked the building, and treat any wall near a leak as a plumbing job first.

## Choosing the Base Material for Maximum Smoothness

The smoothness ceiling is set by the material under the sandpaper, before technique even enters:

**Gypsum skim (bagged).** The finest grain of the common materials, and it sets rather than slowly curing — so it does not develop the shrinkage map-cracking that thick cement passes do. Its weakness is water: any wetting cycle dissolves it. For ordinary interior walls chasing the best finish per hour of labour, gypsum is the standard answer.

**Cement-based skim (cement + fine sieved plaster sand).** Slightly coarser by nature, and its final smoothness depends heavily on the sand: pit-run sand with grains you can see will fight you at every grit; properly sieved, washed plaster sand behaves. Its strengths are cost per square metre, tolerance of slightly damp backgrounds, and compatibility with exterior and wet-area work where gypsum is forbidden. Damp-cure it for 2 to 3 days in hot weather or it will crack on you.

**Multi-filler / ready-mixed repair pastes.** For localized defects, these tub fillers are convenient and fine-grained. They are not wall-surfacing materials — a full wall finished in tub filler costs more than a professional skim, and the thick stacked passes shrink. Use them for the last 5% (corners, chips, crack grooves), not the first 95%.

**The honest combination** most professional interiors use: cement-based base coat where the wall needed real correction, one gypsum skim pass over everything for uniform grain, then putty. Each material does the stage it was engineered for, and no stage is asked to compensate for a skipped one.

## Quick Answers to the Questions People Ask

**How smooth is smooth enough?** The standard worth paying for: no visible ripple or pinhole under raking light, no snag under a bare palm, and no gap over about 2 mm under a 1.5 m straightedge. Anything smoother than that only matters under gloss paint or gallery lighting; matte emulsion in a bedroom will not display the difference you paid for.

**Can I sand my way to smoothness from bad plaster?** No — sanding corrects texture, not geometry. A wall that is bowed 6 mm can be sanded for a week and will remain a bowed wall with sore arms attached. Geometry belongs to skim and re-plastering; sanding only refines what is already true.

**Is machine-finished (sprayed) skim better?** Spray-applied skim is faster and very uniform in thickness, but it still needs the same trowel flattening, sanding, putty, and priming sequence. Machines move the material; they do not remove the stages. Where the marketing says otherwise, the wall will eventually say the truth.

**How soon after skimming can I prime?** When the wall is uniformly light in colour and dry to touch throughout — typically 24 to 48 hours for gypsum, longer in humid weather. Priming over a damp skim seals moisture in; the bill arrives as bubbling and adhesion failure within months.

**Why do my walls ripple only at night?** Because evening bulbs are raking light and daylight is diffuse. The ripple was always there; the light just started grazing it. This is exactly why the torch check during the sequence matters — you are pre-viewing the evening verdict in the afternoon, when fixes are still cheap.

**Does expensive paint hide bad preparation?** It does the opposite. High-sheen and self-levelling premium paints display substrate texture more faithfully than cheap matte. If preparation was skipped, the expensive can makes the wall look worse, faster.

**One coat or two of skim — how do I decide?** Read the wall after assessment: deviations under 2 mm usually need one pass; 2 to 5 mm of ripple wants two passes with the second as a flattening pass; anything deeper is re-plaster territory, not skim. When in doubt, plan two thin passes — the second pass is cheap insurance against the sanding stage exposing hollows that one pass left.

**How long does the full sequence take for one room?** At craft pace, count on five to seven working days including curing: assessment and repairs on day one, skim over the next day or two, sanding and putty a further day, prime, then two topcoats with drying between. Compressing the calendar is how the same room gets done twice in one year.

## Closing

A smooth wall is a sequence, not a product: map the surface, control suction, coat thin twice, sand through grits with the light flat against the wall, remove every grain of dust, prime, and keep the wet edge. For the material quantities this sequence needs on your specific rooms, run the [finishing estimator](/finish-estimator); for choosing between the coating systems, see the [complete guide to interior wall finishing](/learn/complete-guide-interior-wall-finishing).
